{
  "dom_id": "step-10838",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Calculating the Length of a Line Segment With Endpoints on a Circle and Passing Through the Center",
  "problem": "Consider the diagram below. What is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ if $OQ=3?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182550/assets/4e32346cc949b0f17ed219e44d4600abd23a0839a3855fcf76ff4060f322698c.png)",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the points $A,$ $B,$ and $Q$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r =OQ=3.$ \n\nTo find $AB,$ we first notice that\n\n$$\nAB = AO + OB.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overline{AO}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because the radius is $3,$ we have\n\n$$\nAO=3, \\qquad OB=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nAB = AO+OB = 3 + 3= 6.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
